  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Warmest temperatures today and Wednesday.\n\n- Thunderstorms this afternoon south of Interstate 90. Expanding\n  coverage into all of western Montana and north central Idaho on\n  Wednesday. Main threats will be lightning and gusty outflow \n  winds.\n\n- Pacific Northwest trough brings gusty winds later this week and\n  cooler temperatures for the weekend.\n\nHot, dry and smoky conditions will continue through Wednesday as\nhigh pressure remains anchored over western Montana. Monsoon\nmoisture from the Dessert Southwest will be expanding northwards,\nincreasing the chances for thunderstorms in the afternoon and\nevening hours. This afternoon, storms will be most likely along\nand south of Interstate 90. Thunderstorm coverage will expand \nnorthwards to the Canadian border by Wednesday afternoon and\nevening. Due to the very dry low levels of the atmosphere and high\ncloud bases, little precipitation is expected to accompany storms.\nGusty outflow winds will also be a concern with storms, between 30\nto 40 mph today and possibly up to 50 mph on Wednesday. The\ncombination of mostly dry storms, lightning and gusty winds will\nelevate the potential for new fire starts. \n\nOn Thursday, the high pressure ridge begins to break down as a\ntrough of low pressure approaches from the west. Breezy conditions\nare anticipated with another round of thunderstorms, mainly across\nwestern Montana. A subtle drop in temperatures is anticipated by \nFriday as a weak cold front moves through the region. Gusty winds,\ndry conditions and lingering thunderstorms will keep fire weather\nconditions elevated both days.\n\nThe weekend will trend significantly cooler with temperatures at\nor even slightly below normal. There are still a few models \nshowing a more significant low pressure system moving through \nnorthwest Montana, but the majority of models solutions are \ntrending more towards an open wave, which decreases the chance of \nprecipitation. There is still potential to see shower and \nthunderstorm activity in this pattern, but this would be more \nfocused across southwest Montana and Lemhi County.",
